import GL2.Literature.EisensteinIdeal
import Mathlib.NumberTheory.Padics.RingHoms

/-!
# L1: Mazur's Eisenstein ideal, localised at an Eisenstein prime (`hMazurEis`; L-K10, slot 25)

`docs/implementation/slot24/heckez/07-lean-plan.md` §3 (the row `hMazurEis`), 05 §3.1; [C] `prop:heckeZeiscert`(3). The quotations
are those of [GT] `lit:hz-mazur-eis` (read on the **page images** by the LIT agent, 2026-11-04): Mazur, *Modular curves and the
Eisenstein ideal*, Publ. Math. IHÉS 47 (1977), 33–186, Chapter II (printed page = PDF page + 31):

* II §6, p. 90: "By the Hecke Algebra `𝐓` we shall mean the subring of `End(J_{/ℚ})` generated by the Hecke operators `T_ℓ`
  (`ℓ ≠ N`) and by `w`." II §7, p. 91: "`𝐓_𝔞 = lim 𝐓/𝔞^m` denote the completion of `𝐓` at `𝔞`" (a direct factor of `𝐓 ⊗ ℤ_p`).
* II §9, p. 95: "The Eisenstein ideal `𝔍 ⊂ 𝐓` is the ideal generated by the elements: `1 + ℓ − T_ℓ` (all `ℓ ≠ N`) and by
  `1 + w`." p. 96: "Proposition (9.7). — `𝐓/𝔍 = ℤ/n`; the Eisenstein ideal `𝔍` contains the integer `n`." ... "the Eisenstein
  prime corresponding to `p` ... is given by: `𝔓 = (𝔍, p)`."
* II §16, p. 124: "Fix `p` a prime number dividing `n`. Definition. — A prime number `ℓ ≠ N` will be called good (relative to the
  pair `(p, N)` ...) if either: a) not both `ℓ` and `p` are equal to `2`, and (i) `ℓ` is not a `p`-th power modulo `N` and
  (ii) `(ℓ−1)/2 ≢ 0 mod p` or ... b) `ℓ = p = 2`, and `2` is not a quartic residue modulo `N`."
* p. 125: "For any `ℓ` set `η_ℓ = 1 + ℓ − T_ℓ`. ... Proposition (16.1). — The Eisenstein prime `𝔓·𝐓_𝔓 ⊂ 𝐓_𝔓` is generated by the
  elements `p` and `η_ℓ`, where `ℓ` is any good prime. ... Corollary (16.2). — The `ℤ_p`-algebra `𝐓_𝔓` is generated by `η_ℓ` for
  `ℓ` any good prime."
* p. 126: "From now on, in this section, let `p ≠ 2`. ... Proposition (16.6). — The ideal `𝔍·𝐓_𝔓` is a principal ideal in `𝐓_𝔓`,
  generated by `η_ℓ` for `ℓ` any good prime."

**Dictionary** ([GT] `lit:hz-mazur-eis`): Mazur's `p` is our `ℓ`, his good prime `ℓ` is our `q`, his `𝐓_𝔓` is our cuspidal
`𝕋⁰_𝔪` (a `ℤ_ℓ`-algebra). Standing convention II (4.9), p. 77: weight 2, `Γ₀(N)`, `N ≥ 5` prime.

**Interface data:** `T` (Mazur's `𝐓_𝔓`, as a `ℤ_[ℓ]`-algebra), `Tl l` (the image of `T_l`), `w` (the image of `w`). Nothing
here identifies `T` with a Hecke algebra computed by the C++; that is the M-HZ4a block identification.

**The fields.**
* `free`, `finite`: `𝐓_𝔓` is a direct factor of `𝐓 ⊗ ℤ_p` (p. 91), and `𝐓 ⊂ End(J₀(N))` is free of finite rank over `ℤ`.
* `quot` — **a derived consequence of (9.7)**, not a single quotation: `𝐓/𝔍 = ℤ/n` gives `(𝐓 ⊗ ℤ_ℓ)/𝔍 = ℤ_ℓ/n = ℤ/ℓ^{v_ℓ(n)}`,
  and `𝔓 = (𝔍, ℓ)` is the only maximal ideal over `ℓ` containing `𝔍` (`𝐓/(𝔍, ℓ) = 𝔽_ℓ`), so the other local factors of `𝐓 ⊗ ℤ_ℓ`
  are killed by `𝔍` and `𝐓_𝔓/𝔍𝐓_𝔓 ≅ ℤ/ℓ^{v_ℓ(n)}`. Here `𝔍𝐓_𝔓` is `eisensteinIdeal N Tl w` formed in `T`.
* `gen` — (16.2): `T = ℤ_ℓ[η_q]` for every good `q`.
* `principal` — (16.6) (`ℓ ≠ 2`): `𝔍𝐓_𝔓 = (η_q)` for every good `q`.
(16.1) (`𝔓𝐓_𝔓 = (ℓ, η_q)`) and the Gorenstein property (16.3) are not used and not stated.

**Good primes** (`MazurGood`) follow case a) of the definition **literally**: (i) as "no `x ∈ ℤ/N` with `x^ℓ = q`", (ii) as
`((q − 1)/2 : ℤ/ℓ) ≠ 0` (with `2⁻¹` in `ℤ/ℓ`, so `q = 2` is read as Mazur's `1/2 ≢ 0`). Case b) (`ℓ = p = 2`) is outside the
guard `ℓ ≥ 5` and is not encoded. `goodB` (the K10′ test, `GL2/HeckeZ/Exec/K10.lean`) implies `MazurGood`
(`GL2.HeckeZ.K10.goodB_sound`).

**Guards** (`MazurGuard`): `N` prime, `N ≥ 5`, `ℓ ≥ 5` (07's range; Mazur's (16.6) needs `ℓ ≠ 2`), `ℓ ∣ n`. Each field is an
implication from the guard.
-/

namespace GL2.Literature

/-- Mazur's `η_q = 1 + q − T_q`. -/
def mazurEta {T : Type*} [CommRing T] (Tl : ℕ → T) (q : ℕ) : T := 1 + (q : T) - Tl q

/-- A good prime `q` relative to `(ℓ, N)`, Mazur II §16 p. 124, case a), read literally (see the module docstring). -/
def MazurGood (N ℓ q : ℕ) : Prop :=
  q.Prime ∧ q ≠ N ∧ ¬ (q = 2 ∧ ℓ = 2) ∧ (¬ ∃ x : ZMod N, x ^ ℓ = (q : ZMod N)) ∧
    ((q : ZMod ℓ) - 1) * (2 : ZMod ℓ)⁻¹ ≠ 0

/-- The guard of `hMazurEis`: `N ≥ 5` prime, `ℓ ≥ 5` prime (the latter via `Fact`), `ℓ ∣ num((N − 1)/12)`. -/
def MazurGuard (N ℓ : ℕ) : Prop := N.Prime ∧ 5 ≤ N ∧ 5 ≤ ℓ ∧ ℓ ∣ mazurN N

/-- **`hMazurEis`: Mazur, Chapter II, (9.7) localised, Cor. (16.2), Prop. (16.6)** (`VERIFIED` on the page images, [GT]
`lit:hz-mazur-eis`). Interface data: `T` = Mazur's completed cuspidal Hecke ring `𝐓_𝔓` at the Eisenstein prime over `ℓ`, as a
`ℤ_[ℓ]`-algebra, `Tl l` = `T_l`, `w` = the Atkin–Lehner involution. -/
structure MazurEisLocal (N ℓ : ℕ) [Fact ℓ.Prime] (T : Type*) [CommRing T] [Algebra ℤ_[ℓ] T] (Tl : ℕ → T) (w : T) : Prop where
  free : MazurGuard N ℓ → Module.Free ℤ_[ℓ] T
  finite : MazurGuard N ℓ → Module.Finite ℤ_[ℓ] T
  quot : MazurGuard N ℓ → Nonempty (T ⧸ eisensteinIdeal N Tl w ≃+* ZMod (ℓ ^ (mazurN N).factorization ℓ))
  gen : MazurGuard N ℓ → ∀ q, MazurGood N ℓ q → Algebra.adjoin ℤ_[ℓ] {mazurEta Tl q} = ⊤
  principal : MazurGuard N ℓ → ∀ q, MazurGood N ℓ q → eisensteinIdeal N Tl w = Ideal.span {mazurEta Tl q}

/-! ### Sanity instance: the rank-one model `T = ℤ_ℓ`, `η_l = ℓ^v` -/

/-- The quotient of `ℤ_[p]` by `(p^v)` is `ℤ/p^v`. -/
noncomputable def padicQuotPow (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime] (v : ℕ) :
    ℤ_[p] ⧸ Ideal.span {(p : ℤ_[p]) ^ v} ≃+* ZMod (p ^ v) :=
  (Ideal.quotEquivOfEq (PadicInt.ker_toZModPow v).symm).trans
    (RingHom.quotientKerEquivOfSurjective (ZMod.ringHom_surjective (PadicInt.toZModPow v)))

/-- The Eisenstein ideal of the rank-one model is `(ℓ^v)` (any level `N ≠ 2` has the prime `2 ≠ N`). -/
theorem eisensteinIdeal_rankOne (N ℓ v : ℕ) [Fact ℓ.Prime] (hN : N ≠ 2) :
    eisensteinIdeal N (fun l => (1 + (l : ℤ_[ℓ]) - (ℓ : ℤ_[ℓ]) ^ v)) (-1) = Ideal.span {(ℓ : ℤ_[ℓ]) ^ v} := by
  unfold eisensteinIdeal
  apply le_antisymm
  · rw [Ideal.span_le]
    rintro x (hx | ⟨l, -, -, rfl⟩)
    · rw [Set.mem_singleton_iff] at hx; rw [hx]; simp
    · simp
  · rw [Ideal.span_le, Set.singleton_subset_iff]
    exact Ideal.subset_span (Or.inr ⟨2, Nat.prime_two, Ne.symm hN, by ring⟩)

/-- Sanity: `hMazurEis` is satisfiable — by the rank-one model `T = ℤ_[ℓ]`, `T_l = 1 + l − ℓ^v` (every `η_l = ℓ^v`),
`w = −1`, with `v = v_ℓ(n)`, at any level `N ≠ 2` (at `(N, ℓ) = (11, 5)` this is Mazur's `𝐓_𝔓 = ℤ_5`, though the model's
`T_l` are not the eigenvalues of `11a`). -/
theorem mazurEisLocal_rankOne (N ℓ : ℕ) [Fact ℓ.Prime] (hN : N ≠ 2) :
    MazurEisLocal N ℓ ℤ_[ℓ] (fun l => (1 + (l : ℤ_[ℓ]) - (ℓ : ℤ_[ℓ]) ^ (mazurN N).factorization ℓ)) (-1) := by
  have hI := eisensteinIdeal_rankOne N ℓ ((mazurN N).factorization ℓ) hN
  have hη : ∀ q, mazurEta (fun l => (1 + (l : ℤ_[ℓ]) - (ℓ : ℤ_[ℓ]) ^ (mazurN N).factorization ℓ)) q =
      (ℓ : ℤ_[ℓ]) ^ (mazurN N).factorization ℓ := fun q => by unfold mazurEta; ring
  refine ⟨fun _ => inferInstance, fun _ => inferInstance, fun _ => ⟨?_⟩, fun _ q _ => ?_, fun _ q _ => ?_⟩
  · rw [hI]; exact padicQuotPow ℓ _
  · rw [eq_top_iff]; intro x _
    exact (Algebra.adjoin ℤ_[ℓ] _).algebraMap_mem x
  · rw [hI, hη]

end GL2.Literature
